--  Saving what a script worked out.
--
--  Everything that wrote a file before these existed wrote what a *program*
--  had printed -- `run_into` and its relatives attach a program's output to a
--  file -- so a value the language itself computed had nowhere to go. A shell
--  that can read a file, run a program and work something out, and then cannot
--  save the answer, has to end every script by piping through `cat`.
--
--  These are commands rather than functions, and that is deliberate. `Exists`
--  and its two companions are functions because a question has no consequences
--  and may safely sit inside a condition. Writing has consequences, so it is a
--  statement, where a reader sees it happen: nobody should be able to write
--  `if Exists (P) and then Write (P, "") then` by accident.

--  Everything below writes into the working directory, so run this somewhere
--  it may leave four files behind -- `cd` first if that is not where you are.
--  Asking a program for a scratch directory is what this used to do, and it
--  meant the example only ran on hosts that ship `mktemp`.

--  Replaces whatever was there.
write_file ("first", "report.txt");
write_file ("second", "report.txt");

--  Read_File is write_file's other half: text out, and no program started to
--  fetch it.
put_line ("the file holds: " & Read_File ("report.txt"));

--  Adds to the end, and makes the file when it is not there yet -- the first
--  turn of a loop that collects lines is not an error.
for Attempt in 1 .. 3 loop
   append_file (Trim (Attempt'Image) & " ", "log.txt");
end loop;

put_line ("the log holds: " & Read_File ("log.txt"));

--  A write that worked says nothing. A command that announced each one would
--  put its own lines into the script's output, and then nothing could read
--  what the script itself printed. `Status` says what became of it, in the
--  same way it does for a program that ran.
write_file ("x", "no-such-directory/out.txt");

if Status /= 0 then
   put_line ("that one had nowhere to go");
end if;

--  Which makes the shape a script uses to save its work only when it can.
procedure Save (Text : String; Where : String) is
begin
   write_file (Text, Where);

   if Status = 0 then
      put_line ("saved");
   else
      put_line ("not saved");
   end if;
end Save;

Save ("keep this", "kept.txt");
Save ("keep this", "nor-this-directory/kept.txt");

--  Taking things away again is `remove_file` and its neighbours, which
--  `directories.adash` uses. Unmaking was left to programs for a long time, on
--  the grounds that unmaking things is what programs are for -- and that
--  stopped being true when this shell began running where those programs are
--  not there to be run.
